<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>8.11.2: Finding a Quadratic Function from Its Zeros</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02273</md:content-id>
    <md:title>8.11.2: Finding a Quadratic Function from Its Zeros</md:title>
    <md:uuid>bdd0a480-c3b4-4915-8496-4084f72a2f12</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section>
      <title>Activity</title>
      <para>In previous lessons, you learned about the relationship between a quadratic function's zeros and the binomial factors in the factored form of the equation. Use this information to answer the following questions. If needed, review activities 8.9.3 and 8.10.3.</para>
      <para>Questions 1 – 3 refer to a quadratic equation that has the zeros –3 and 4. </para>
      <para>Use the "^" symbol to enter exponents.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para> Write the factored form of this equation set equal to zero. </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the factored form of the equation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\((x + 3)(x − 4) = 0\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Now, write the standard form of the same quadratic equation.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the standard form of the equation.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(x^2 − x − 12 = 0\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Write the quadratic equation as the function \(f(x)\).</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter the quadratic equation as the function.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − x − 12\)</para>
          <para>So a quadratic equation that has the zeros –3 and 4 can be expressed as a function \(f(x) = x^2 − x − 12\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <para>For questions 4 – 8, identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(f(x)\), with the given zeros.</para>
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>8 and 4</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 12x + 32\)</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x − 8)(x − 4)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>−6 and 3</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 + 3x − 18\)</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x + 6)(x −3)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>\(\sqrt3\) and \(-\sqrt3\)</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 3\)</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x + \sqrt3)(x − \sqrt3)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>5</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 10x + 25\)</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x − 5)(x − 5)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>−4</para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 + 8x + 16\)</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x + 4)(x + 4)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Examine the graph below, then identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(f(x)\), with the given zeros.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 4 AND 6.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_13_03_01.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 2x − 24\)</para>
          <para>The zeros are –4 and 6.</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x + 4)(x − 6)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Examine the graph below, then identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(f(x)\), with the given zeros. </para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH AN \(x\)-intercepts OF 3.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_13_03_02.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 6x + 9\)</para>
          <para>The zero is 3.</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zero is \(f(x) = (x − 3)(x − 3)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
      <!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise>
        <problem>
          <para>Examine the graph below, then identify a quadratic function in standard form, \(f(x)\), with the given zeros.</para>
          <para>
            <figure>
              <media alt="A PARABOLA THAT OPENS UP WITH \(x\)-intercepts OF NEGATIVE 2 AND 5.">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_09_13_03_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
            </figure>
          </para>
        </problem>
        <solution>
          <para>Enter a quadratic function in standard form.</para>
          <para>Compare your answer:</para>
          <para>\(f(x) = x^2 − 3x − 10\)</para>
          <para>The zeros are –2 and 5.</para>
          <para>The factored form derived from the zeros is \(f(x) = (x + 2)(x − 5)\).</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <!--Interaction End -->
    </section>
  </content>
</document>
